Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': "Whispers in Edinburgh's Old Town", 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Edwardian Mysteries', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'The Discovery', 'text': "Yet Eleanor Ashford kept returning to a ship's manifest, the way one returns to a sore tooth. It told a story — of a debt unpaid, a promise broken, a life ruined. And stories, Eleanor Ashford was beginning to understand, are a kind of debt themselves.\n\nThe a ship's manifest came to Eleanor Ashford on a rainy Thursday in Edinburgh's Old Town, tucked in a solicitor's envelope smelling of dust and secrets. Eleanor Ashford was not, by nature, a curious person. Curiosity, in Edinburgh's Old Town, was a luxury for those with full larders.\n\nBut a ship's manifest would not be ignored. That night, by gaslight, Eleanor Ashford examined it: the handwriting, the seal, the date — twenty years gone. And the name. The name that the blackmailer had paid good money to bury."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'Inquiries', 'text': "Eleanor Ashford began carefully, as one must in Edinburgh's Old Town: a question to the vicar, a look at the parish register, a conversation with the old soldier who remembered everything and forgave nothing.\n\nthe old soldier proved unexpectedly brave, carrying messages, watching doors, learning the servants' gossip that the masters never hear. 'The downstairs knows everything,' the old soldier said. 'Upstairs just pays for it.'\n\nThe daguerreotype — no, a ship's manifest — revealed its last secret then: a face in the background, a witness the blackmailer had missed. Eleanor Ashford found the address. The game, as the young people say, was afoot — though in Edinburgh's Old Town, they said it more politely."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'The Witness', 'text': "The witness lived in Edinburgh's Old Town's poorest court, old and afraid and tired of being afraid. a ship's manifest in Eleanor Ashford's hands loosened their tongue: yes, they had seen. Yes, they would swear. No, they had never dared.\n\nEleanor Ashford promised protection — a promise that felt like a stone in the pocket, heavy and necessary. the old soldier arranged the safe room, the food, the watchers. Edinburgh's Old Town's poor take care of their own; they always have.\n\nthe blackmailer struck that night: a break-in, a fire set to frighten, a thug with a message written in bruises. the old soldier took the worst of it and joked through split lips. Eleanor Ashford wept and planned."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "Eleanor Ashford stood in the emptying hall with a ship's manifest and felt not triumph but tiredness, and under it, peace. character is destiny. It had cost plenty. It was worth it.\n\nthe blackmailer laughed — then didn't. The evidence was a chain, each link forged: a ship's manifest, the register, the witness, the ledger page the old soldier had risked everything to copy. Link by link, it held.\n\nIt came to a head at the assembly rooms, in front of all Edinburgh's Old Town: Eleanor Ashford stood and spoke, with a ship's manifest held high and the witness beside them. The gaslight seemed to lean in to listen."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Restoration', 'text': "Eleanor Ashford went back to ordinary life, which turned out to be extraordinary enough: work, friends, Sundays, the small heroisms of kindness. a ship's manifest had taught them that history is just people, choosing.\n\nEdinburgh's Old Town changed, the way places do when a bad tooth is pulled: slowly, then all at once. The mill got new owners. The court got a new magistrate. a ship's manifest got a place of honor — and then, deliberately, a quiet drawer.\n\ncharacter is destiny — Eleanor Ashford had it carved, finally, on no monument at all, just lived it, in Edinburgh's Old Town, in every ordinary, extraordinary day."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Coda: The Drawer', 'text': "character is destiny. In Edinburgh's Old Town, in every place, in every age. The end — which is to say, the beginning.\n\nthe blackmailer is a name in the records, a caution. Edinburgh's Old Town is a place where the mills run fair and the magistrate is honest, mostly, which is as much as any age can ask.\n\nThe gaslight is electric now. The carriages are cars. But a ship's manifest is still a ship's manifest, and Eleanor Ashford is still Eleanor Ashford, and the drawer still opens."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

